};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "gnc protein powder",
      "gnc amp wheybolic",
      "gnc pro performance whey",
      "gnc protein review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">GNC Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      GNC's house-brand protein is built around AMP Wheybolic, relaunched in 2026 with a 25 g scoop and a 50 g two-scoop serving. Here is what changed, which GNC product to buy, and when a mainstream whey is the better deal.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#the-brand">Who Makes GNC Protein</a>
      <a href="#label">What the AMP Wheybolic Label Says</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#fifty-grams">Do You Need 50 g (Two Scoops) in One Shake?</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#range">Wheybolic, Ripped, Shakes and Pro Performance</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Clinical Claims</a>
      <a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#vs-rivals">GNC vs Optimum Nutrition vs Dymatize</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          GNC AMP Wheybolic is a solid whey, and its 2026 relaunch as Wheybolic Original makes it a standard one: per GNC's label, each scoop gives 25 g of protein in 120 calories, close to Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard's scoop. The "50G" on the tub is two scoops. Judge it on cost per 25 g of protein, not grams per serving, and buy it when GNC's sale price beats Optimum Nutrition or Dymatize.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            GNC AMP Wheybolic is a competent whey protein powder. It is easy to find in GNC stores, widely discounted in the chain's frequent sales, and the 2026 Original label lists 25 g of protein, 1 g of sugar and 1 g of fat per 120-calorie scoop. Nothing on the label is a red flag, and buyers generally report the flavors are easy to drink.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is the serving size on the front of the tub. "50G Whey Protein" is a two-scoop serving, double what most whey labels list for one scoop. Gram for gram, AMP Wheybolic is about as protein-dense as any other good whey blend, not more. A tub with 26 servings of 50 g holds the same protein as a tub with 52 servings of 25 g, so the only honest way to compare it is the cost per 25 g of protein. Older tubs list 40 g per two scoops; the 2026 Original lists 25 g per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            On that measure, it depends entirely on the price you pay. At GNC's regular shelf price, AMP Wheybolic is usually more expensive per gram than Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard or Dymatize ISO100 on Amazon. During GNC's frequent promotions, it can be competitive. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder rankings</a> list the brands that are cheaper per gram most of the time.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the verdict is simple. Buy AMP Wheybolic if you shop at GNC anyway and you catch it on sale. Use one scoop instead of two if you do not need 50 g at once. Buy a mainstream whey on Amazon if you want the best price per gram every day, or a certified isolate if you compete in drug-tested sport.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A standard 25 g scoop with a two-scoop label. Compare it per 25 g, not per serving.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">AMP Wheybolic is worth buying on a GNC sale. At full price, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard or Dymatize ISO100 usually costs less per gram of protein.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="the-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes GNC Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            GNC, originally General Nutrition Centers, is one of the oldest supplement retailers in the US. It grew from a single health food store opened in Pittsburgh in 1935 into a national chain of stores, and it has sold its own house-brand supplements for decades alongside third-party brands like Optimum Nutrition and Dymatize. After filing for bankruptcy in 2020, the company was bought by Harbin Pharmaceutical Group, a Chinese pharmaceutical company that was already a major investor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Its protein powders sit in several house lines. AMP, short for Advanced Muscle Performance, is the performance line, and AMP Wheybolic is its flagship whey. GNC Pro Performance is the long-running mainstream sports line, which includes a basic 100% Whey. GNC also sells weight-management and specialty proteins under other names, and the line-up changes as products are reformulated and renamed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because GNC owns both the brand and the stores, its protein is priced and promoted differently from brands sold everywhere. Shelf prices are often high, but the chain runs frequent sales, member pricing and bundle deals, so the price you actually pay can vary widely week to week. That matters more for GNC protein than for almost any other brand when you work out value.
          </p>

          <p>
            We have not tasted or tested GNC's proteins ourselves. This review is built from GNC's product labels as published on its listings, retailer listings and nutrition-tracker entries that copy the label. Taste and mixing notes are buyer feedback. GNC's site blocked automated access when we checked, so where we could not confirm a figure, such as the current Pro Performance whey label, we say so instead of guessing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What the AMP Wheybolic Label Says
          </h2>

          <p>
            GNC relaunched AMP Wheybolic in 2026 as Wheybolic Original, sold as New Wheybolic. GNC's own label for Chocolate Fudge (revised October 2025) lists, per 32.4 g scoop, 120 calories, 25 g of protein, 3 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar, 0 g of fiber, 1 g of fat, 115 mg of sodium and 3.1 g of L-leucine. Per two-scoop serving of 64.7 g it lists 240 calories, 50 g of protein, 5 g of carbohydrate, 2 g of sugar, 1.5 g of fat, 230 mg of sodium and 6.2 g of leucine. The 59.4 oz (3.7 lb, 1,683 g) tub holds 52 scoops, or 26 two-scoop servings, and the front reads "Wheybolic Original 50G Whey Protein."
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">AMP Wheybolic Original (2026)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 scoop, 32.4 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3.1 g leucine, 52 scoops per 59.4 oz tub</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">AMP Wheybolic Original (2026)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 scoops, 64.7 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">240</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6.2 g leucine, 26 servings per tub</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">AMP Wheybolic Ripped</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 scoops</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 200</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 22 servings; per GNC's listing at the time of writing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">AMP Wheybolic 40 shake</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 bottle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 190</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 6 g carbs, 1 g sugar; per GNC's listing at the time of writing</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those numbers translate to 25 g of protein per 32.4 g scoop, or about 77 percent protein by weight. That is typical of a good isolate-led whey blend: a little below a pure isolate, which tends to run closer to 80 to 90 percent, and in line with mainstream blends such as Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard. The older formula was closer to 65 percent protein, which is why the new scoop carries more protein for a similar weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            GNC markets AMP Wheybolic with leucine on the front of the tub, which is a feature of every whey, since whey is naturally rich in leucine. The label lists 3.1 g of L-leucine per scoop and 6.2 g per two-scoop serving. A single scoop already clears the 2 to 3 g often cited as enough to maximize the muscle-building response to a meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Chocolate Fudge ingredient list is whey protein isolate, whey protein concentrate, cocoa powder (processed with alkali), natural and artificial flavors, sucralose, salt, sunflower lecithin, cellulose gum, guar gum, xanthan gum, steviol glycosides and silicon dioxide. It contains milk, and the label says no artificial colors, gluten free and no soy. Because it includes concentrate, it carries some lactose, and our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate guide</a> explains why that matters. Our <a href="/wheybolic-protein-powder-review/">full Wheybolic review</a> compares the old and new formulas in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. GNC Protein Products and Alternatives, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            GNC sells several house-brand proteins, but AMP Wheybolic is the line it builds around. The three GNC products below are the ones whose figures we could find in GNC's labels and listings at the time of writing. Two clearly labelled alternatives from other brands follow, because for many buyers a mainstream whey is the better buy on Amazon. GNC products are sold mainly through GNC stores and gnc.com, so check the seller before buying them elsewhere.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="fifty-grams" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Do You Need 50 g (Two Scoops) in One Shake?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most people, no. Research on protein timing generally finds that around 20 to 25 g of high-quality protein, or about 0.25 to 0.4 g per kg of body weight, captures most of the muscle-building response to a single meal for many people. One 25 g scoop of Wheybolic Original hits that on its own. A 50 g two-scoop shake is not wasted, because the extra protein still counts toward your daily total, but the extra muscle benefit from the second scoop is usually modest.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are exceptions. A study by Macnaughton and colleagues in 2016 found that doubling a post-workout whey dose from a 20-gram to a 40-gram serving after a whole-body resistance workout produced a larger muscle-building response in trained young men. Larger lifters, people training many muscles in one session, and older adults, who tend to need more protein per meal to get the same response, may all reasonably use a bigger serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            What matters far more is your total daily protein. Many guidelines for people who train suggest roughly 1.6 g per kg of body weight a day, spread over three or four meals. If a two-scoop shake helps you reach that number easily, it is doing its job. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a> and <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">timing guide</a> walk through the numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is that you control the serving. If 50 g is more than you need, use one scoop and the 26-serving tub gives you 52 shakes, which makes the per-shake price look much better. If you are a large lifter trying to hit 180 g or more a day, the two-scoop serving is a convenient way to get there. Either way, the scoop size does not make the protein better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-per-25g" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because AMP Wheybolic's label serving is two scoops, its price per serving always looks high. The fair comparison is cost per 25 g of protein, and with the 2026 Original that is easy: one scoop is 25 g. The 59.4 oz tub holds 52 scoops, about 1,300 g of protein, so divide the price by 52. The 28 oz tub holds 13 two-scoop servings, or 26 scoops, so divide by 26. At the $64.99 Amazon price we saw for GNC's 28 oz Classic Vanilla tub, that is about $2.50 per 25 g, which is pricey.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers (59.4 oz tub)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">52</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Scoops per tub</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~1,300 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per tub</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Price / 52</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cost per 25 g</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            We are not quoting a single GNC store price because it moves so much. GNC's shelf prices change often, and the chain runs regular sales and member deals that can cut the price sharply. Check the actual price on the day, then run the calculation. Mainstream wheys like Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard came in at about $1.49 per 25 g in a 5 lb tub at the Amazon price we saw, which is the benchmark AMP Wheybolic needs to beat. Our <a href="/wheybolic-protein-powder-review/">full Wheybolic review</a> tracks the Amazon and GNC prices for each tub size.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ready-to-drink AMP Wheybolic 40 shake is a different calculation. Bottled protein always costs more per gram than powder, because you are paying for the liquid, the bottle and the convenience. Use it for days when you cannot mix a shake, not as your everyday protein. Our <a href="/best-protein-drink/">best protein drink guide</a> compares bottled shakes on cost and label.
          </p>

          <p>
            If price per gram is your main priority, our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">cheap protein powder guide</a> ranks powders purely on cost per 25 g. GNC's own proteins rarely top that list at regular prices, but a good sale can put AMP Wheybolic in the same range as the best-value mainstream tubs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Wheybolic, Ripped, Shakes and Pro Performance
          </h2>

          <p>
            AMP Wheybolic is the flagship and the one to buy if you are choosing a GNC protein. The 2026 Wheybolic Original lists 25 g of protein and 120 calories per scoop, or 50 g and 240 calories per two scoops, in flavors including Chocolate Fudge and Classic Vanilla. Old-formula tubs may still be on some shelves, so check the label before you buy. Buyers report picking it for the flavor choice and GNC's sales. Our <a href="/wheybolic-protein-powder-review/">full Wheybolic review</a> covers every size and flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            AMP Wheybolic Ripped is the cutting-focused version. Per GNC's listing at the time of writing, it gives 40 g of protein in about 200 calories per two-scoop serving, with about 22 servings per tub. Next to the Original's 50 g in 240 calories, that is fewer calories but also less protein, about 5 calories per gram of protein against 4.8, so it is not actually leaner. Versions marketed for cutting often include extra ingredients, so read the label. For most people, one scoop of the Original or a plain isolate does the same job in a calorie-controlled diet.
          </p>

          <p>
            The AMP Wheybolic 40 ready-to-drink shake comes in 4-packs and 12-packs. Per GNC's listing at the time of writing, it gives 40 g of protein, about 190 calories, 1.5 g of fat, 6 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of sugar per bottle. That is a strong bottled-shake profile, with more protein than most ready-to-drink options, which typically run 20 to 30 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            GNC Pro Performance 100% Whey is the long-running budget-leaning line many people search for. We could not confirm its current label from a source we could access, so we have not given it a product card or figures here. If you see it in a store, compare it with AMP Wheybolic on cost per 25 g using the label on the tub. Our <a href="/pure-protein-powder-review/">Pure Protein review</a> and <a href="/muscle-milk-protein-powder-review/">Muscle Milk review</a> cover other mainstream wheys in the same price tier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Testing and Clinical Claims
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 2026 Wheybolic Original label carries the Informed Choice logo, and GNC markets it as tested for banned substances. We could not confirm the product in the Informed Choice registry ourselves, so treat the logo as a strong sign rather than proof: the label carries the Informed Choice logo; check the registry for your batch. Programs like Informed Choice and NSF Certified for Sport test for banned substances and are the standard drug-tested athletes should rely on.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you compete under drug testing, search the Informed Choice, Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport databases for the exact product, flavor and batch before you buy, whichever brand you choose. Dymatize ISO100 and many Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard flavors are commonly cited with Informed Choice certification, per those brands, so they are worth checking too.
          </p>

          <p>
            GNC also markets AMP Wheybolic as clinically proven to increase muscle strength and support muscle building. We could not review the study behind that claim, so we cannot tell you how it was designed or what it compared. More broadly, research consistently shows that adequate protein, including whey, supports strength and muscle gains when combined with resistance training. That is true of good whey in general, not a unique property of one product.
          </p>

          <p>
            For heavy metals and contaminants, whey generally tests cleaner than plant proteins and cocoa-heavy products, though chocolate flavors of any protein tend to carry more lead than vanilla. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains what testing claims mean and what to ask a brand for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip AMP Wheybolic if you mainly want the cheapest protein per gram and do not shop at GNC. At regular shelf prices, mainstream wheys sold on Amazon are usually cheaper per 25 g of protein, and our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">whey protein rankings</a> name the best-value options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hold off if you compete in a drug-tested sport until you have found your batch in the Informed Choice registry; the label carries the logo, but we could not confirm the listing ourselves. Skip it, or test your tolerance carefully, if you are lactose intolerant, since the protein includes whey concentrate alongside isolate. A pure isolate is the safer choice for lactose sensitivity. Skip it too if you avoid sucralose or gums: the label lists sucralose, stevia and three gums.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip the 50 g two-scoop serving if you are a smaller person, eat plenty of protein already, or are watching calories closely. One 25 g scoop is enough for many people per shake, and you can halve the serving without any downside. Using two scoops by default adds 120 calories a day you may not need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Anyone with kidney disease, who is pregnant or breastfeeding, or who takes medication should talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before using a high-protein supplement regularly. This review is not medical advice. Healthy adults generally tolerate whey protein well at normal doses, but a 50 g serving is a large single dose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. GNC vs Optimum Nutrition vs Dymatize
          </h2>

          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey is the benchmark mainstream whey. Per the label at the time of writing, a scoop delivers about 24 g of protein in roughly 120 calories, from a blend led by whey isolate, and Optimum Nutrition promotes Informed Choice certification on many flavors. In the large 5 lb tubs it is usually one of the cheapest per gram among the major brands. Our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition review</a> covers the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dymatize ISO100 is the isolate alternative. It lists about 25 g of protein in roughly 120 calories per scoop with about 1 g of sugar, per the label, and Dymatize states it carries Informed Choice certification. It is the better pick for lactose-sensitive buyers and tested athletes, and it usually costs a little more per gram than Gold Standard. Our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize review</a> compares ISO100 with Elite Whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compared with those two, AMP Wheybolic Original is no longer the big-scoop outlier. Its 25 g, 120-calorie scoop is a standard size, almost the same as Gold Standard's, so the old serving-size argument is gone. Its real advantages are good pricing during GNC sales, the store convenience and the Informed Choice logo on the label. Its sweetening is sucralose plus stevia with three gums, which suits some buyers and not others. Its disadvantage is a regular price that is usually higher per gram, especially on Amazon.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sensible approach is to buy whichever of the three is cheapest per 25 g of protein on the day, unless you need certification, in which case choose ISO100 or a certified Gold Standard flavor. For bigger-picture comparisons, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-muscle-gain/">muscle gain protein powder guide</a> and <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">whey isolate rankings</a> go further.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing grams per serving instead of per gram</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                AMP Wheybolic Original's 50 g serving is two scoops, so a tub of 26 servings holds the same protein as a tub of 52 servings at 25 g. Always divide the price by total grams of protein and multiply by 25. That is the only fair way to compare it with other whey.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying full price at GNC</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                GNC's shelf prices are often high, but the chain runs frequent sales, bundles and member deals. Buying AMP Wheybolic at regular price usually makes it more expensive per gram than mainstream whey on Amazon. Wait for a promotion, or buy a rival that is cheaper on the day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using two scoops by default</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most people get most of the muscle-building benefit of a meal from around 20 to 25 g of protein. Two scoops of Wheybolic Original adds 120 calories and halves the life of the tub. Start with one scoop and use two only after big full-body sessions or if you need the extra protein to hit your daily target.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a logo means your batch is certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A logo or a listing that says tested is not the same as finding your product in the certifier's registry. If you compete under drug testing, search the Informed Choice, Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport database for the exact product, flavor and batch before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the shake as your main protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The ready-to-drink AMP Wheybolic 40 has a strong label, but bottled shakes cost far more per gram than powder. Keep a few for travel and busy days and get your everyday protein from food and powder. Anyone with kidney disease should check high-protein products with a doctor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

